# Launcher

## Overview

A status screen with hostname, time, battery and IP addresses, refreshed
every two seconds. **Screen off**, or a press on the power button, cuts
the backlight and blacks out the screen; the next power button press
wakes it instantly (a tap works too, but isn't the main way in — cutting
the backlight also kills touch). The screen also blanks on its own after
a period of no touch (`screen_blank_timeout` in `config.exs`). **Suspend**
puts the whole phone to sleep, only from this button; wake it with the
power key, then tap once touch is back.

The code is `NervesLivebookFP3.Launcher` in
`lib/nerves_livebook_fp3/launcher.ex`. Edit it there and load it into the
running phone with `scripts/hot-load.sh`.

## Start at boot

The firmware starts the launcher at boot unless autostart has been turned
off for this phone. It's on by default on a freshly flashed phone:

```elixir
NervesLivebookFP3.Launcher.autostart(false)
```

```elixir
NervesLivebookFP3.Launcher.autostart?()
```

## Start and stop by hand

```elixir
NervesLivebookFP3.Launcher.start()
```

```elixir
NervesLivebookFP3.Launcher.stop()
```

After a stop, Emerge can't start another viewport until the phone reboots.

## Check power support

Suspend needs `mem` in the first list. The second shows which kind of
suspend `mem` means; the one in brackets is used.

```elixir
{File.read!("/sys/power/state"), File.read!("/sys/power/mem_sleep"), File.ls("/sys/class/backlight")}
```
